    <h2>1. The Four Pillars of Soundproofing</h2>
    <p>
      In architectural acoustics, every high-performance wall assembly relies on four distinct physical mechanisms:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1. Mass:</strong> Heavy materials (such as 5/8" Type X drywall or concrete) resist acoustic movement through inertia.</li>
      <li><strong>2. Decoupling:</strong> Mechanically separating wall layers (staggered studs, double studs, or resilient sound clips) stops sound from vibrating through solid wood framing.</li>
      <li><strong>3. Absorption:</strong> Mineral wool or fiberglass inside stud cavities absorbs airborne reverberation.</li>
      <li><strong>4. Damping:</strong> Viscoelastic materials dissipate sound wave vibration energy before it can travel through solid surfaces.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. How Constrained Layer Damping (Green Glue) Works</h2>
    <p>
      Green Glue operates on a patented phenomenon called <strong>Constrained Layer Damping (CLD)</strong>. When sound waves hit the first layer of drywall, the sheet flexes slightly, creating rapid microscopic shearing forces across the surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because Green Glue sits sandwiched between two rigid sheets of drywall without hardening, this rapid shear motion forces the long-chain polymers inside the compound to stretch and slide against one another. This internal friction converts mechanical kinetic sound energy directly into microscopic amounts of harmless <strong>thermal energy (friction heat)</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most importantly, Green Glue completely flattens the natural <em>coincidence frequency resonance dip</em> (typically 2,000 to 3,500 Hz), where standard gypsum partitions ring like a hollow bell and let human speech pass through.
    </p>

    <h2>3. How Mass Loaded Vinyl (MLV) Works</h2>
    <p>
      Mass Loaded Vinyl is a dense, flexible PVC material heavily impregnated with high-specific-gravity minerals (usually naturally occurring barium sulfate or calcium carbonate). A 1/8-inch thick sheet of 1 lb/sq ft MLV provides the mass of a solid sheet of lead without the toxicity.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unlike rigid drywall, MLV is a <strong>limp mass barrier</strong>: when sound waves strike it, it does not resonate or ring. However, MLV is notoriously difficult to install in residential framing:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A 4 ft x 25 ft roll weighs 100 to 200 lbs, requiring two people and heavy mechanical pneumatic staplers to hang.</li>
      <li>Every vertical and horizontal seam must be overlapped and sealed with specialized PVC vinyl seam tape.</li>
      <li>If MLV is sandwiched tightly between two layers of drywall, its limpness is constrained, reducing its acoustic isolation benefit.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Head-to-Head Architectural Comparison</h2>
